    {
      "id": "node-comp-1",
      "type": "comparison",
      "content": {
        "text": null,
        "level": null,
        "markdown": null,
        "description": null,
        "subtitle": null,
        "title": "Why Continuity is Necessary but Not Sufficient",
        "latex": null,
        "equation": null,
        "formula": null,
        "expression": null,
        "annotation": null,
        "id_tag": null,
        "label": null,
        "highlight": null,
        "step_number": null,
        "explanation": null,
        "left_title": "Necessity (Differentiable $\\implies$ Continuous)",
        "left_points": [
          "If $\\lim_{h \\to 0} [f(a+h) - f(a)] = \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h} \\cdot h = f'(a) \\cdot 0 = 0$.",
          "This forces $\\lim_{h \\to 0} f(a+h) = f(a)$, which is the exact definition of continuity at $x = a$."
        ],
        "right_title": "Insufficient Counterexample (e.g., $f(x) = |x|$ at $x = 0$)",
        "right_points": [
          "The function $f(x) = |x|$ is continuous at $x = 0$ because $\\lim_{x \\to 0^+} |x| = \\lim_{x \\to 0^-} |x| = f(0) = 0$.",
          "However, Left-Hand Derivative (LHD) is $-1$ while Right-Hand Derivative (RHD) is $+1$. Since LHD $\\neq$ RHD, $f'(0)$ does not exist due to a sharp corner."
        ],
        "callout_type": null,
        "canvas_type": null,
        "purpose": null
      },
      "importance": "critical",
      "layout_preference": "full_width"
    },
